CAMSV capture driver for MT6895 (Redmi K50 / rubens)
===================================================

cam_cap.c is a self-contained CAMSV1 capture driver for the IMX582 rear camera
on the MT6895 (Redmi K50, codename rubens).

Capture path
------------

  IMX582 -> CSI-2 port 2 -> SENINF intf 4 -> SENINF mux 1 -> CAM_MUX 3
         -> camsv1@1a110000 IMGO DMA -> V4L2 /dev/video0 (YUYV 422)

The driver owns the sensor I2C bus (bus 10, address 0x10) for runtime exposure,
gain and open-loop DW9800V focus writes, programs SENINF / CAM_MUX / CAMSV,
allocates the capture buffer with dma_alloc_coherent() from CMA and maps it
through the display IOMMU.  Raw frames rotate through a three-slot capture
pipeline, so the arm thread does not wait for the converter to release the
previous slot; conversion itself runs on a worker pool whose threads are niced
so the pool cannot starve the rest of the system.  A per-frame governor on the
conversion thread drives AE and AWB; /proc/camcap exposes statistics and
commands.

Sensor modes
------------

imx582_modes.h carries five IMX582 mode tables (preview 4000x3000,
normal_video 4000x2256, custom3 4000x2256@60, custom2 1920x1080@120,
hs_video 1920x1080@240).  The file is generated by scripts/gen_modes_header.py
in the companion repository listed below.  The driver replays a table itself
when the mode changes:

  echo modes > /proc/camcap                  list the tables
  echo "mode custom2 1" > /proc/camcap       switch to 1920x1080, binning 1

The same switch is reachable from V4L2:

  v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 --set-fmt-video=width=1920,height=1080,pixelformat=YUYV
  v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 --set-parm=240

VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES and VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMEINTERVALS report the tables; the
frame rate selects between tables of one size.  Modes that change the MIPI data
rate (custom3 and hs_video run at 1964 Mbps/lane, the others at 1370) need the
CSI receiver retimed.  The driver recomputes D-PHY HS_TRAIL and CSI2 DMY_CYCLE,
the two fields of the receiver setup that follow the rate (rx_rate=1).

The boot-time power-up sequence and the initial mode come from the bring-up
scripts in the companion repository.

Enable and load
---------------

  CONFIG_VIDEO_MTK_CAMSV=m

  insmod cam_cap.ko v4l2_enable=1 pipe_slots=3 pipeline=1

conv_threads defaults to 8 (the workers are niced to 10 and the pool only runs
while a frame is converted), pipe_slots selects the capture pipeline depth
(2..4, default 3; three measured fastest).  The converter is compiled with -O3
for the same reason.

Useful parameters: v4l2_bin (1 = full size output, 2 = 2x2 binned),
v4l2_full_cache, exp_hsize/exp_vsize/v4l2_src_stride, exp_max, out_width/
out_height, rb_swap, pipeline, pipe_slots, conv_threads, ae_enable/awb_enable/
af_enable, dphy_base, rx_rate, mode_trace, mode_init_replay.  See the module's
MODULE_PARM_DESC strings.

Reproducible builds
-------------------

The module the phone runs reports

  srcversion: 493F61FF760E440C2CC5AA7
  vermagic:   7.2.0-g0b8dd2e87b3d-dirty SMP preempt mod_unload aarch64

The companion repository holds the kernel revision as the annotated tag
k50-camera-base (0b8dd2e87b3d), the kernel configuration and a one-shot script
that reproduces both values without a full kernel build.  See
docs/REPRODUCIBLE_BUILD.md there.

Notes, mode tables and bring-up scripts
---------------------------------------

  https://github.com/rubens-mt6895-mainline/k50-camera

The sensor register tables the init sequence is based on come from the vendor
kernel (MiCode/Xiaomi_Kernel_OpenSource, rubens-s-oss branch).
